Concrete execution now matches both prompt examples. I’m moving from examples to the general argument: the proof target is a loop invariant over an arbitrary algebraic sequence of words and arbitrary accumulated output, with a separately defined oracle for “append this word iff its length is one of the primes ≤100.” This is what lets `kprove` cover unbounded word counts instead of merely replaying test cases.
